---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import AskList from '../components/AskList.astro';
import ToolCta from '../components/ToolCta.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../components/Disclaimer.astro';
import { pageByPath } from '../lib/pages';
import { articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd } from '../lib/jsonld';
import { questionsToAskEnd, generalFaqs } from '../data/general';

const page = pageByPath('/questions-to-ask-at-the-end-of-an-interview/')!;
---
<Base title={page.title} description={page.description} path={page.path} jsonLd={[articleJsonLd(page), faqJsonLd(generalFaqs.questionsToAsk)]}>
  <h1>Questions to ask at the end of an interview</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    "Do you have any questions for us?" is not a formality — it is the last impression you make, and often the
    moment you learn the most about the job. "No, I think you covered everything" is the one answer that always
    lands badly. Have three or four of these ready.
  </p>
  <AskList items={questionsToAskEnd} />
  <p>
    One of these deserves special attention: asking whether anything in your background gives the interviewer
    pause. It feels risky, but it is the only move that lets you answer a concern while you are still in the
    room — concerns left unspoken simply become reasons.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the full set, grouped by topic, see <a href="/questions-to-ask-in-an-interview/">questions to ask in an
    interview</a>.
  </p>
  <ToolCta heading="Save your closing questions in a prep pack" />
  <Faq faqs={generalFaqs.questionsToAsk} />
  <Disclaimer />
</Base>
